# /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.d/core.rules
# The base's own token-saving command rules (shipped by ai-tools-base). Parsed, never sourced, by
# /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.lib.sh, which carries the grammar and the safety rules; this
# file carries only the per-command judgement.
#
#   match<TAB>action<TAB>blocking<TAB>payload
#
# Loaded FIRST, so a provider's rule set can override any rule here.
#
# Each rule below narrows how much a command PRINTS, never what it inspects, and cancels itself
# the moment the agent passes a flag of its own about the same thing. Three commands qualify;
# git diff, git show, git blame and grep are deliberately absent, because every terse mode they
# have discards content the agent asked for by running them.

# The default log entry is six lines per commit -- commit/Author/Date/blank/indented subject --
# where one carries the same facts. No count is imposed: `git log -5` must keep meaning five.
git log	args	--format,--pretty,--oneline,--graph,-p,--patch,--stat,--numstat,--shortstat,--name-only,--name-status,-L,-S,-G	--date=short --format='%h %ad %an %s'

# The long form pads each file with instructional hints ("use git restore ...") the agent already
# knows; --branch keeps the ahead/behind line, which is the part it reads.
git status	args	-s,--short,--porcelain,--long,-v,--verbose,-z,--column	--short --branch

# An unbounded tree of a repository with a package directory in it is the single largest
# accidental output a session produces. The depth cap and the file limit both announce themselves
# in tree's own output, so a truncated branch is visible rather than silent.
tree	args	-L,--filelimit,-J,-X,--du	-L 3 --filelimit 100
